\description{
Bring a saved Stan/brms summary (and, when available, its checksum-bound
\code{posterior-draws.rds}) back into mfrmr for shared-calibration contrasts,
arbitrary equal-tailed credible levels, figures and reports without loading
either sampling backend or running an estimator.
}
\details{
Run the exported bundle's \verb{Rscript run.R --summarize} first.
Current \code{summarize.R} writes both \code{posterior-summary.rds} and
\code{posterior-draws.rds}, binding them by MD5 checksums to \code{model-data.rds}.
The reader checks the recorded model-data and summary checksums, parameter
mappings, fixed-value semantics, free-to-constrained affine/Helmert basis
reconstruction, and consistency of the saved sampling checks. Older
summaries without a checksum (or without \code{posterior-draws.rds} when joint
draws are needed) can be regenerated with the current \code{run.R} and
\code{summarize.R} using \code{--summarize}; no new sampling is needed. Keep the
original \code{model-data.rds} bytes with the fit when moving an export directory.

When only \code{posterior-summary.rds} is present, the object contains marginal
95\% summaries, not joint posterior draws: parameter covariance is never
fabricated from marginal SDs, and requests for contrasts, other credible
levels, or empirical draw plots fail closed. When \code{posterior-draws.rds} is
attached, \code{summary(x, contrasts = ..., level = ...)}, \code{confint()}, \code{plot()},
\code{as.data.frame()}, and \code{\link[=mfrm_report]{mfrm_report()}} evaluate linear or ratio contrasts
directly across the shared chain-by-iteration posterior draws while checking
algebraic constraint bases (\code{fixed_anchor} and \code{fixed_by_constraint} roles
are determined from the exported basis matrices, never from sample variance).
Posterior SDs, equal-tailed credible intervals, and tail probabilities
(\code{prob_gt_zero}, \code{prob_lt_zero}) are Bayesian summaries conditional on the
model, priors, and fixed anchors; they are not frequentist standard errors,
Wald confidence intervals, two-sided p-values, or MML likelihood-ratio tests.

Estimated-population one-family Stan summaries retain the population mean
and SD as reported parameters. Their schema-2 files require this newer reader;
existing fixed-population schema-1 summaries remain supported. Use
\code{plot(x, quantity = "population_sd")} to inspect between-Person variation
on the native metric. This SD is not an individual Person standard error.

Fixed anchors and constraint-determined values have no estimated SD or
interval (\code{NA}). External calibration uncertainty has not been propagated.
\code{summary()} returns the validated object (recomputing credible bounds or
attaching \verb{$contrasts} when requested); printing is compact.
The bundled synthetic examples use short chains and require sampling
review; their numbers are workflow illustrations, not assessment findings.

\code{plot()} method: Plot saved posterior estimates, contrasts, and credible intervals

For \code{plot()}: These are equal-tailed \emph{posterior credible} intervals
at \code{level}, not frequentist confidence intervals. Dashed intervals and open
circles mark estimates whose sampling screen requires review; solid
intervals mean that no sampling flag was detected, not that inference is
validated. Open diamonds mark fixed anchors/constraint-determined values
without an interval. Crosses mark estimated points without an available
interval. Missing points retain their labels and are never replaced by zero.
Original row order, labels and endpoints are preserved.
}
